<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Preserving Our Heritage – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.2em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Preserving Our Heritage</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What Should We Preserve in the Future?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Specific Objective:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Listening: Receptive: Summarize detailed explanations in academic lectures. Interactive: Participate in group discussions on literature.</p>
            <b>Learning Outcome:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Listening: Can summarize the main ideas in a lecture about a historical Panamanian figure (e.g., "The speaker talked about Victoriano Lorenzo, a Panamanian hero who fought for independence. He was a symbol of resistance against foreign control.").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can participate in discussions on literature, sharing insights from a story (e.g., "In the story, the Emberá villages are depicted as a vital part of Panama's heritage.").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can respond constructively to classmates' views on cultural heritage by sharing examples and asking questions to deepen the discussion (e.g., "I agree that the Emberá weaving tradition is important. What do you think about how it's passed down to younger generations?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images featuring iconic Panamanian historical sites (e.g., Casco Viejo, Panama Viejo, the Panama Canal, Emberá villages, traditional Ngäbe-Buglé attire). Students work in pairs to identify what they see and briefly discuss what these places/items represent to Panama. The teacher then writes the words "heritage," "culture," and "preserve" on the board and asks students what they mean in relation to the images. (5 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary words like "heritage" (/ˈher.ɪ.tɪdʒ/) emphasizing the reduced vowels, "preserve" /prɪˈzɜːrv/, "trade" /treɪd/, and "became" /bɪˈkeɪm/, focusing on the long /eɪ/ sound. The teacher explains the meaning of "heritage" as "something inherited from the past, valuable enough to pass on." Example: "The Panama Canal is part of our national heritage, which we must preserve." (5 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'heritage' something old or something new?" (Expected answer: Something old, from the past.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If we 'preserve' something, do we keep it safe or destroy it?" (Expected answer: Keep it safe.)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher provides further examples: "Our ancestral traditions are part of our cultural heritage." "We must make efforts to preserve our historical sites." (5 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio excerpt (approx. 2-3 minutes) from an academic lecture titled "Victoriano Lorenzo: A Panamanian Legacy and Identity." The lecture uses the grammar structures of relative clauses, past simple vs. present perfect, and future simple, and incorporates core vocabulary: <i>heritage, history, landmark, legacy, identity, preserve, became, efforts, traditional, culture</i>. The register is formal and academic, suitable for 11th grade.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understanding spoken messages in various contexts related to cultural and historical significance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (First Listen - Gist):</b> The teacher instructs students to listen for the main idea. (2 min)</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the main topic of this lecture?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who is Victoriano Lorenzo, according to the speaker?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Second Listen - Specific Information): The teacher provides a handout with True/False statements or multiple-choice questions related to key facts and opinions expressed in the lecture. For example:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Victoriano Lorenzo fought for Panama's independence from Colombia." (T/F)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"His legacy has always been celebrated consistently throughout Panamanian history." (T/F)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The speaker believes that we will continue to preserve his memory through educational efforts." (T/F)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students listen again and mark their answers. (8 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice identifying and summarizing information from detailed explanations, focusing on relative clauses, past simple vs. present perfect, future simple, and the target vocabulary (<i>heritage, preserve, history, landmark, legacy, identity, efforts, traditional, culture, attract, sites, became</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Listening Cloze:</b> The teacher provides a transcript of a portion of the lecture with 8-10 key vocabulary words and grammatical structures (e.g., relative clauses, verb tenses) removed. Students listen to the audio excerpt a third time and fill in the blanks. Example sentence from transcript: "Victoriano Lorenzo, ________ became a symbol of resistance, led indigenous groups in a fight for justice, ________ was a critical part of our national identity. His actions ________ many followers. Historians ________ him as a key figure." (<i>which, who, attracted, have remembered</i>) (5 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Sentence Scramble & Listen:</b> The teacher gives students scrambled sentences from the lecture that contain target grammar (relative clauses, past simple/present perfect) and vocabulary. Students try to unscramble them in pairs. Then, they listen to the same audio segment again to check their answers and verify word order and content. Example: "a / around / World / Site, / tourists / attracts / Casco Viejo, / UNESCO / is / which / from / Heritage / the / world." (Correct: "Casco Viejo, which is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, attracts tourists from around the world.") (5 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Peer Summary Check:</b> In small groups (3-4 students), students share their answers from the previous activities. They then collaboratively summarize the main points of the lecture, ensuring they use at least 5-6 of the target vocabulary words (<i>heritage, history, legacy, preserve, identity, efforts</i>) and some of the practiced grammar (e.g., "Victoriano Lorenzo became a relevant historical figure, who..."). They practice asking about cultural significance: "Why is preserving his legacy important for our Panamanian identity?" (5 min)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Discussion following a literary audio excerpt.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> The teacher plays a short audio excerpt (approx. 2 minutes) from a story or folk tale that depicts a traditional Panamanian community or cultural practice (e.g., "The Emberá Villages: Keepers of Tradition" focusing on weaving, traditional dances, or unique living structures). This audio segment is less academic and more narrative, preparing students for literary discussion. It will embed words like <i>villages, weaving, traditional, culture, live, offer</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">After listening, students engage in a small group discussion (4-5 students) about the story. Each group receives a discussion prompt: "What aspects of the Emberá heritage, as depicted in the story, do you think are most important to preserve for the future? How do these traditions contribute to Panama's identity?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are encouraged to share insights from the story, use the target vocabulary (e.g., "In the story, the Emberá villages are depicted as a vital part of Panama's heritage."), and practice polite discussion language (e.g., "I agree that the Emberá weaving tradition is important. What do you think about how it's passed down to younger generations?"). They should use relative clauses and future simple to express opinions and predictions (e.g., "The Emberá community, which lives in harmony with nature, will continue to offer unique cultural experiences to tourists."). (10 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' ability to summarize the main ideas from the academic lecture and participate constructively in the literary discussion, sharing insights and responding to classmates' views on cultural heritage.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can summarize the main ideas from the lecture (e.g., who Victoriano Lorenzo was, his significance).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses at least 4-5 target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>heritage, legacy, identity, preserve, traditional</i>) appropriately in the discussion.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates intelligible pronunciation, especially for /eɪ/ and reduced vowels in multisyllabic words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participates respectfully and constructively in the group discussion, asking questions and responding to peers' views.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to use relative clauses (e.g., "which," "where") and future simple correctly.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a brief exit ticket before leaving:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"List three new things you learned about Panamanian heritage today from listening."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel summarizing a lecture after today's lesson? (1-5, 5 being very confident)"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of listening to the academic lecture or the story today, and why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can listening to more examples of Panamanian cultural heritage help you understand its importance?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in engaging students with the topic of heritage and the listening activities? The use of visual aids for warm-up and varied audio inputs (lecture, story) seemed effective. Students showed good initial engagement with the topic.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students struggled with distinguishing past simple from present perfect in faster speech. I will need to provide more explicit examples or a quick review in the next lesson if it's relevant, or perhaps a pre-reading activity for Lesson 2 that specifically targets these structures. Ensure the selected audio texts are not overly long to maintain focus. Provide more opportunities for listening to key phrases multiple times.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2, students will read a text about Panamanian heritage sites, building on the vocabulary and themes introduced today. This will allow them to see the same core vocabulary (<i>heritage, history, sites, culture, preserve, attract, landmark, efforts</i>) in a new context and deepen their understanding before moving to speaking and writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>